Summary (one-liner)

This API (GET /claims/{universalClaimAggregationId}) takes a Universal Claim Aggregation ID, validates it, checks its existence in Neptune, fetches all related source-system claim IDs (SRKs), applies System Of Record (SOR) rules to deduplicate true SORs using the latest update timestamp, and returns a clean list of claim systems — or 404 if the claim does not exist.


---

High-level flow

1. Controller receives universalClaimAggregationId as path variable.


2. Service validates the ID format and domain.


3. Neptune is checked to confirm the vertex exists.


4. Service fetches all SRKs linked to the aggregation.


5. For each SRK:

Resolve its System Of Record (SOR).

Build a ClaimSystem object.

Deduplicate true SORs by keeping the latest updated record.



6. Build and return GetClaimSystemsByIdResponse.


7. If claim not found → return 404.




---

Step-by-step explanation (simple, complete, concise)

1) Controller / endpoint

Endpoint:
GET /claims/{universalClaimAggregationId}

Input:

universalClaimAggregationId → canonical ID for Universal Claim Aggregation.


Controller calls:

claimService.getClaimSystemsById(universalClaimAggregationId);

If service returns null → 404 Not Found (Claim not found).

If response exists → 200 OK with list of claim systems.



---

2) Service entry — getClaimSystemsById(String id)

First step is ID validation:

new GmsIdentifierClaimAggregation(universalClaimAggregationId);

This ensures:

Correct domain prefix (UNIVERSAL_CLAIMS_AGGREGATION)

Valid UUID format


Invalid ID → DataFormatException (technical error).



---

3) Check if claim exists in Neptune

neptuneService.vertexWithIdExists(gmsIdentifierClaimAggregation);

What happens internally

Executes query:

MATCH (n {~id: $id})
RETURN count(n) as count

If count > 0 → claim exists.

If count = 0 → log info and return null.


📌 Why this step exists
Prevents unnecessary graph traversal and ensures clean 404 behavior.


---

4) Fetch related system IDs (SRKs)

findRelatedVertices(
  gmsIdentifierClaimAggregation,
  Relationship.HAS_SYSTEM_ID
);

Returns all SRKs linked to this claim aggregation.

Each SRK represents:

One source system’s claim ID.




---

5) Resolve System Of Record (SOR) per SRK

getSystemOfRecord(srkVertex);

Traverses SRK → OF_SOR relationship.

Enforces exactly one SOR per SRK:

If 0 or >1 SOR found → ApplicationException (data issue).


Reads SOR code and converts to SystemOfRecord enum.



---

6) Build ClaimSystem object

For every SRK:

Extract:

Source system name (from SOR)

Source claim ID (ORDERED_KEY_VALUES)

Source ID type (derived from SOR)

Last updated timestamp (UPDATED_DATE)


Build:

ClaimSystem {
  source,
  sourceId,
  sourceIdType,
  lastUpdatedDate
}

lastUpdatedDate is internal only (@JsonIgnore).



---

7) True SOR deduplication logic (core business rule)

Some systems are marked as True SOR.

There can be multiple SRKs from the same true source due to updates.


Deduplication approach

For each True SOR:

Compare UPDATED_DATE

Keep only the latest record


Non-True SORs:

Added directly without deduplication.



📌 Outcome
One authoritative claim ID per true source system, no duplicates, no stale data.


---

8) Build and return response

GetClaimSystemsByIdResponse.builder()
  .claims(claimSystems)
  .build();

Response contains

List of ClaimSystem objects:

source

sourceId

sourceIdType


No timestamps exposed to API consumers.



---

9) Error handling & logging

Invalid aggregation ID → DataFormatException

Claim not found in Neptune → return null → 404

Multiple SORs per SRK → technical ApplicationException

Neptune query failure → error logged, safe fallback



---

10) Key business rules (why they matter)

Existence check first → clean 404 semantics.

Strict SOR enforcement → protects data integrity.

True SOR deduplication → avoids duplicate and outdated claim IDs.

Hidden internal fields → clean, consumer-friendly API.



---

One-line takeaway (for manager)

> This API resolves a Universal Claim Aggregation ID into all associated source-system claim IDs by validating the claim, traversing Neptune relationships, enforcing strict System Of Record rules, and returning a deduplicated, authoritative list of claim systems.




